te formula to write $A^{-1}$.","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}4&-2\\\\-3&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix}4&-2\\\\-3&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{pmatrix}4&-2\\\\-3&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$-2\\begin{pmatrix}4&-2\\\\-3&1\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f4bd7b3e88bc4de2e8d1be0a","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch9_adjugate_matrix_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\det(A)=0$, what remains true about $\\operatorname{adj}(A)$?","options":["$\\operatorname{adj}(A)$ automatically equals the inverse.","$\\operatorname{adj}(A)$ is defined, but the inverse formula fails.","$\\operatorname{adj}(A)$ is undefined for singular matrices.","$\\operatorname{adj}(A)$ must be the zero matrix."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3389c74a96c5e36b62b751ec","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch9_adjugate_matrix_010_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}$ be a $3\\times3$ matrix with $\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})=0$. What is $\\boldsymbol{A}\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{I}_3$, as the adjugate acts like an inverse","The $3\\times3$ zero matrix","Undefined, as $\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})$ needs $\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})\\ne0$","A matrix with zero diagonal but possibly nonzero off-diagonal entries"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"62e8c84a25c9833194fad11e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_adjugate_matrix_e15dd508_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2&0\\\\0&1&3\\\\4&0&1\\end{pmatrix}$, with cofactors $C_{ij}=(-1)^{i+j}\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}_{ij})$, where $\\boldsymbol{A}_{ij}$ deletes row $i$ and column $j$. What is the entry in row $1$, column $2$ of $\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})$?","options":["$12$","$-2$","$2$","$-12$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ee74767d81dea8143cfb6da6","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_adjugate_matrix_e15dd508_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}$ be a $3\\times3$ real matrix with $\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})=2$. Using $\\boldsymbol{A}\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})=\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})\\boldsymbol{I}_3$, what is $\\det(\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A}))$?","options":["$2$","$8$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c7341d3d8584f522e73aee5a","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_adjugate_matrix_e15dd508_topup20261028_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}$ be any $3\\times3$ real matrix, singular or not. How is $\\operatorname{adj}(3\\boldsymbol{A})$ related to $\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})$?","options":["$\\operatorname{adj}(3\\boldsymbol{A})=9\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})$","$\\operatorname{adj}(3\\boldsymbol{A})=3\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})$","$\\operatorname{adj}(3\\boldsymbol{A})=27\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})$","$\\operatorname{adj}(3\\boldsymbol{A})=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{adj}(\\boldsymbol{A})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cee1e945be75cf5a7100ed5a","time_limit":30}]}}